>
  <div class="docs-content">
    <h1>Exporting Data</h1>
    <p class="docs-summary">
      Premium users can export their BudgetBaby data as CSV files for spreadsheet analysis or as a
      formatted PDF financial summary. Free users get one CSV export per month.
    </p>

    <h2 id="formats">Export formats</h2>
    <h3>CSV</h3>
    <p>
      A CSV export produces <strong>up to four separate files</strong>, one each for transactions,
      categories, recurring bills, and subscriptions, depending on what you have. The files are
      packaged together so you can open whichever one you need in any spreadsheet app.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Date format.</strong> ISO 8601 with full date (YYYY-MM-DD), parsed reliably by Excel, Numbers, and Google Sheets.</li>
      <li><strong>Amount format.</strong> Pure numeric values with no currency symbol. A separate Currency column carries the code so sorting and arithmetic work correctly.</li>
      <li><strong>Encoding.</strong> Plain UTF-8.</li>
      <li><strong>Priority labels.</strong> Categories use their display names: <em>Essentials</em>, <em>Variable</em>, <em>Fun</em>, <em>Unplanned</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Payment method labels.</strong> Capitalised display names, <em>Cash</em>, <em>Debit</em>, <em>Credit</em>, <em>Savings</em>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>PDF</h3>
    <p>
      The PDF export is a formatted financial summary report, not a raw data file. It's designed
      for sharing or printing, use CSV when you need the underlying data.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-mocks-row">
